<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilona Zrínyi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1703">1703</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jelena Zrinska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilona Zrínyi was a Croatian-Hungarian noblewoman, widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> Rákóczi and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>. In 1682 she married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a>. She defended the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukachevo">Munkács</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army for three years until 1688, and died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilona-zrinyi.jpg" alt="Ilona Zrínyi" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilona_Zr%C3%ADnyi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ilona Zrínyi was born in 1643 at Ozalj, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, the eldest child of Petar Zrinski, Ban of Croatia, and his wife Katarina Zrinska, née Frankopan, a Croatian poet. She and her siblings Judita Petronila, Aurora Veronika and Ivan Antun were the last generation of the once-powerful Zrinski family. Little is known of her schooling, but she was known for her beauty and learning, gained from her parents, both writers, and from her uncle Nikola VII Zrinski.</p><p>On 1 March 1666 she married Francis Rákóczi. Their children were György, who died in infancy, Julianna, born in 1672, and Ferenc, known as Francis Rákóczi II, born in 1676. Her husband died on 8 June 1676, and against the advice of Emperor Leopold I's counsellors and her husband's own will she gained guardianship of the children and with it control of the Rákóczi estates, among them the castles of Regéc, Sárospatak, Makovica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukachevo">Munkács</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a> she took an active part in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> uprising against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. When the Ottoman forces and their Kuruc allies were defeated at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683, Thököly lost one Rákóczi castle after another, and by the end of 1685 the imperial army had surrounded the last, Munkács Castle, also called Palanok, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. She defended it in person against the forces of General Antonio Caraffa, and was pregnant during the siege.</p><p>After the recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> her position became untenable, and on 17 January 1688 she surrendered, on the understanding that the defenders would be amnestied and the Rákóczi estates kept in her children's names. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, against the agreement, the children were taken from her. She lived in the Ursuline convent until 1691, while her son was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> school at Neuhaus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Thököly captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> general Heissler, a prisoner exchange freed her, and she joined her husband in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 both went into exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. She lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a>, a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and later in Izmit, where she died on 18 February 1703; she was buried in the church of Saint Benoit in Galata.</p><p>She is honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as a national heroine who opposed the absolutist aims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, a struggle her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a> carried on from 1703 to 1711. In October 1906 her remains were reinterred with his in St Elisabeth Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilona Zrínyi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His marriage to <b>Ilona Zrínyi</b>, widow of Francis I Rákóczi, brought him the great Rákóczi estates and the castle of Munkács.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ozalj, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.61,15.48&amp;year=1682" aria-label="Ozalj on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 556 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ilona Zrínyi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5367 826h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5367 826h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.61,15.48&amp;year=1682">What was here in 1682? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
